| Modifier              | Same Class | Same Package   | Child (Different Package) | Outside Package (Non-child) |
| --------------------- | ---------- | -------------- | ------------------------- | --------------------------- |
| `private`             | ✅          | ❌            | ❌                         | ❌                       |
| `protected`           | ✅          | ✅            | ✅                         | ❌                       |
| `public`              | ✅          | ✅            | ✅                         | ✅                       |
| default (no modifier) | ✅          | ✅            | ❌                         | ❌                       |


Private → Data hiding (strong encapsulation)
    Used when you don’t want subclasses touching internal data.

Protected → Designed for inheritance
    Used when you want subclasses to reuse or modify behavior.
    protected members cannot be accessed via parent object reference in a different package.
    protected has two access paths:
    1⃣ Same package → behaves like default (package-private)
    2⃣ Different package → accessible only through inheritance
